## Summary
The article argues for the defense of academic freedom, emphasizing that harsh critics should not be punished even when their actions lead to tragic outcomes. It references Nathan Cofnas in this context, advocating for the protection of free inquiry in academia.

## Key Points
- Academic freedom is a principle that must not be abandoned or suppressed.
- Individuals should not face punishment for being harsh critics, regardless of the consequences.
- Tragic results do not justify compromising on the values of open debate and criticism.


